nforce zero commutativity; that is, ensure that every invalid choice results in an immediate dead end.\n\n\nAnalyzing a new move sequence to confirm a single solution, the setup is `[Đ, V, V]`, `[X, V, Đ, Đ]`, `[X, X, V]`, and Slot `[X]`. At this stage, all columns can move. Only column 2 leads to the solution. The other moves lead to immediate deadlocks. The single move is to move `V` from column 1 to slot. The key focus now is the subsequent state and ensuring the next move is unambiguous and deterministic.\n\n\nThe previous level, while seemingly single-solution, overlooked the critical \"Empty Column\" rule.  A cleared column immediately becomes empty, allowing any card to move there, thus creating alternate solution paths.  This makes the game non-deterministic and compromises the single-solution constraint. This \"empty column\" rule needs to be factored into the next design. The key is now to ensure every invalid move forces an immediate, unrecoverable deadlock or invalid game state, by carefully managing column clearance.\n\n\nThe core challenge is now to design a level where an incorrect move into the empty column after the initial color clear immediately results in an unrecoverable deadlock. Considering a scenario where, after the first color (`Đ`) clears, Column 1 is empty, and Column 2 has `[X,X,V,V,V]` (bottom `VVV`), while Column 3 has remaining cards, how to ensure any move *other* than the intended one leads to an inescapable block? The goal now is to prevent non-solution moves.\n\n\nAnalyzing a new strategy for ensuring a single solution, the focus shifts to preventing moves into the empty column after an initial clearance. The objective is to design a situation where any move to the slot immediately leads to a deadlock. Investigating the setup where the player clicks the slot rather than the intended column, resulting in the slot receiving the last available card, which makes the only card available to click into an empty column and thus block. This forces a single-solution by ensuring an immediate unrecoverable state if any move other than the intended one occurs.\n\n\nThe key insight is that moving three `V`s from a column with `[X, X, V, V, V]` to an empty column provides the single solution because no other `V`s exist, forcing their exit. A move to the holding slot would only take one `V`, leaving the other `V`s blocked. All other moves immediately deadlock the game by forcing an invalid state. This ensures that every invalid choice creates an unrecoverable deadlock, enforcing a unique solution by construction. The empty column condition, when exploited, is key for the designed single unique solution.\n\n\nThe previous design failed because the empty column created ambiguity. The revised approach centers on ensuring an immediate, unrecoverable deadlock if the wrong move is made. By strategically blocking cards, the level forces a specific solution.  Specifically, if the player clicks the Slot, and makes Column 1 empty first by moving three cards to clear the last `Đ`s, the game immediately ends in a no-win state, as the cards are now locked. Backtracking to identify the starting level reveals Cột 3 must have cards to maintain a three-column game. Need to backtrack fully from the desired deadlock. The column must NOT be empty after step 1.\n\n\nWith three columns and four colors, an unrecoverable deadlock can be designed if clearing a specific column leaves only one empty. If the first clear removes one color, we now have three colors remaining, with 9 cards total across 3 columns and the slot. To make only *one* empty column, all remaining cards must be distributed strategically, ensuring other moves lead to an immediate and impossible state, such as blocking the last move to clear all cards. Now to begin the level construction with this new concept, where any invalid move creates a terminal, unrecoverable state.\n\n\nThe strategy now aims to create an unrecoverable state if any move *other* than the single solution path is taken. With three columns and four colors (three cards per color), the focus is on a scenario where clearing one column (e.g., Column 3) leaves it empty. Column 1 then would have `[Purple, Purple]` and Column 2 would contain `[Purple, Green, Green, Yellow, Yellow, Yellow]`. The Slot would hold `[Green]`.\n\nExamining the possible clicks from that state: Clicking Column 1's `[Purple, Purple]` yields a useless swap. Clicking the Slot (Green) means no column is open anymore, and is a no-win condition.\n\n\nThe goal is to design a single-solution level where every move, at every stage, is uniquely forced and non-reversible, preventing any possibility of alternative solutions or deadlocks except along the correct path. This demands mathematical certainty. A new level with a state graph is being developed and will be tested to be completely non-backtracking and always forced at every single move.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"node -e \"\nfunction getBottomGroupCount(col) {\n  if (col.length === 0) return 0;\n  const c = col[col.length - 1];\n  let k = 0;\n  for (let i = col.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n    if (col[i] === c) k++; else break;\n  }\n  return k;\n}\n\nfunction findColTarget(columns, color, startIdx) {\n  const n = columns.length;\n  for (let s = 1; s < n; s++) {\n    const idx = (startIdx + s) % n;\n    if (columns[idx].length > 0 && columns[idx][columns[idx].length - 1] === color) return idx;\n  }\n  for (let s = 1; s < n; s++) {\n    const idx = (startIdx + s) % n;\n    if (columns[idx].length === 0) return idx;\n  }\n  return -1;\n}\n\nfunction findSlotTarget(columns, color) {\n  for (let i = 0; i < columns.length; i++) {\n    if (columns[i].length > 0 && columns[i][columns[i].length - 1] === color) return i;\n  }\n  for (let i = 0; i < columns.length; i++) {\n    if (columns[i].length === 0) return i;\n  }\n  return -1;\n}\n\nfunction stepState(state, action, targetCount) {\n  const columns = state.columns.map(c => [...c]);\n  let slot = state.slot;\n  if (action.type === 'col') {\n    const idx = action.idx;\n    const col = columns[idx];\n    if (col.length === 0) return null;\n    const k = getBottomGroupCount(col);\n    const color = col[col.length - 1];\n    const t = findColTarget(columns, color, idx);\n    if (t !== -1) {\n      col.splice(col.length - k, k);\n      for (let i = 0; i < k; i++) columns[t].push(color);\n      if (columns[t].length === targetCount && getBottomGroupCount(columns[t]) === targetCount) {\n        columns[t] = [];\n      }\n      return { columns, slot };\n    }\n    if (!slot) {\n      col.pop();\n      slot = color;\n      return { columns, slot };\n    }\n    return null;\n  } else {\n    if (!slot) return null;\n    const t = findSlotTarget(columns, slot);\n    if (t === -1) return null;\n    const color = slot;\n    slot = null;\n    columns[t].push(color);\n    if (columns[t].length === targetCount && getBottomGroupCount(columns[t]) === targetCount) {\n      columns[t] = [];\n    }\n    return { columns, slot };\n  }\n}\n\nfunction serialize(state) {\n  return JSON.stringify(state);\n}\n\nfunction isWin(state) {\n  return !state.slot && state.columns.every(c => c.length === 0);\n}\n\n// Find all simple (no repeated state) winning paths\nfunction findAllSimpleWinPaths(initialState, targetCount, maxDepth = 12) {\n  const paths = [];\n  function dfs(state, visited, path) {\n    if (isWin(state)) {\n      paths.push(path);\n      return;\n    }\n    if (path.length >= maxDepth) return;\n    const actions = [];\n    for (let i = 0; i < state.columns.length; i++) actions.push({ type: 'col', idx: i, label: 'C' + (i + 1) });\n    actions.push({ type: 'slot', label: 'Slot' });\n    for (const a of actions) {\n      const next = stepState(state, a, targetCount);\n      if (!next) continue;\n      const key = serialize(next);\n      if (visited.has(key)) continue;\n      visited.add(key);\n      dfs(next, visited, [...path, a.label]);\n      visited.delete(key);\n    }\n  }\n  const initKey = serialize(initialState);\n  dfs(initialState, new Set([initKey]), []);\n  return paths;\n}\n\n// Test random 3-col, 3-color, 3-cards-per-color configurations\nconst cards = ['R','R','R','Y','Y','Y','B','B','B'];\nfunction shuffle(a) {\n  for (let i = a.length - 1; i > 0; i--) {\n    const j = Math.floor(Math.random() * (i + 1));\n    [a[i], a[j]] = [a[j], a[i]];\n  }\n  return a;\n}\n\nconst seen = new Set();\nfor (let iter = 0; iter < 50000; iter++) {\n  const s = shuffle([...cards]);\n  const cols = [s.slice(0,3), s.slice(3,6), s.slice(6,9)];\n  if (cols.some(c => getBottomGroupCount(c) === 3)) continue;\n  const init = { columns: cols, slot: null };\n  const key = serialize(init);\n  if (seen.has(key)) continue;\n  seen.add(key);\n  const paths = findAllSimpleWinPaths(init, 3, 12);\n  if (paths.length === 1) {\n    console.log('FOUND EXACTLY 1 SIMPLE PATH:', JSON.stringify(cols), 'Path:', paths[0].join(' -> '));\n    break;\n  } else if (paths.length > 0 && paths.length <= 2) {\n    console.log('Paths count:', paths.length, JSON.stringify(cols), paths.map(p => p.join('->')));\n  }\n}\n\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolSummary":"Search for unique-solution puzzle configuration"}}]}
